\begin{eqnarray}
\sup_{\{r_{ij}\},\{\nu_i\}}
\left\{ \sum_{i,j=1,i\neq j}^K
r_{ij}\log v_{ij} + \sum_{i=1}^K \mu_i\nu_i
- I\big(\{r_{ij}\},\{\nu_i\}\big) \right\}.
\nonumber\\
\end{eqnarray}
In terms of the conjugate variables, an off-diagonal $q_{ij}$ simply serves as a scaling for $v_{ij}$, and a diagonal $q_{ii}$ serves as a reference value for $\mu_i$. The statistics of the rate of changes is captured by the $\lambda$, as a Hamiltonian function.
